Test data of kourier-telegram (TG-2), CC0-1.0.

login-flow.jsonl is what TDLib 1.8.68 (the pinned commit then; LC-20 moved the pin to 1.8.69 and kept
this file as it is: it is the SPIKE's fixture, the lane cannot re-record it, and the auth states and
login functions it shows are identical at 1.8.69; the fake-DC login cases prove the login live at the
pin) wrote through td_json while the
TDLib spike (spikes/tdlib-t0) logged in against its own fake data centre, received a message
and sent one: 176 objects, no network of Telegram's involved. It was SCRUBBED before it came
here: every phone number is one of two synthetic ones (+999068204719 for the own account,
999075390162 for the peer), the names are Ada Test and Bo Peer (chat titles too), every message
text is "synthetic text <n>", signatures, sender tags and client data are blank, the t_me_url
option is https://example.invalid/, the request names (@extra) are gone, the UTC offset is 0
and every date is moved to the fixed synthetic epoch 1700000000 (the seconds between the dates
are the flow's own).

The scrub is scripts/tg-fixture-scrub.py (python3 -I scripts/tg-fixture-scrub.py
<spike-flow.jsonl> <out-dir>; it writes both files here). It asserts that no original name,
number, title, username, signature, text or date survives (it prints the counters, all 0) and
that every string-valued key path is on the reviewed list string-paths.txt. tests/fixture.rs
re-checks the RESULT against the same list (an unknown string path fails
`the_fixture_is_scrubbed`), the dates and the offset, and that the list holds no stale path;
the originals themselves are only in the script's run, so "no original survives" is the
script's check, not the test's.

string-paths.txt is the reviewed list of string-valued key paths (one per line); a new kind of
text in a later fixture is looked at by a person before it is added.

login-rules.jsonl holds the scripted TDLib's rules (`kourier_tdlib::scripted::Script::
from_fixture`, with the `when` and `nth` keys of TG-2) cut from that flow: the synthetic number
answered by WaitCode, any other number by PHONE_NUMBER_INVALID, the synthetic code 12345 by the
flow's own updates up to Ready, any other code by PHONE_CODE_INVALID, and getMe by the own user.

rec-<name>.jsonl (TD-4a, fifteen files: fresh-start, restart-saved-pts, kill-at-once, kill-after-quiet,
reconnect, gap-known-sender, gap-filled-by-push, gap-channel, gap-unknown-sender, channel-difference, edit,
delete, read-elsewhere, incoming-call, basic-group-upgrade) are what TDLib 1.8.69 (first recorded at 1.8.68;
LC-20 re-recorded all 19 files) with patch 0001+0002 (the
test-only fake-DC library) wrote through the helper while the lane's fake DC
(crates/kourier-telegram/fakedc/, its standard synthetic world) served the recording cases
RC1-RC11 (RC5 in four cases a-d) of src/supervisor/tests/fakedc/rec.rs, inside an `unshare -rn` child: no network of
Telegram's involved. Each holds TDLib's own lines after the login (Ready onwards), the app's
request markers ({"@type":"kourierRequest","request":<type>,"@extra":<n>}, plus "args" with the
request for loadChats, getChatHistory, getMessage, getChat, getChats, viewMessages and getMe only;
never setTdlibParameters or a login input) and the case's step lines
({"@type":"kourierStep","step":<label>}). `@extra` is RENUMBERED 1..n per file, so an answer
pairs with its marker. Made by `cargo xtask tdlib fakedc record` (raw files under
target/tg-fakedc/raw/, never committed) and scrubbed by
`python3 -I scripts/tg-fixture-scrub.py --recording <raw> --world <world.json> <out-dir>`: the
fake DC's originals (its world.json) become Ada Test / +999068204719 (own), Bo Peer /
999075390162 and Cy, Di, Ed, Fay Sample (peers, by id), `Group <n>` / `Channel <n>` (titles),
`synthetic_<n>` (usernames), `synthetic text <n>` (texts, one number per original text, so an
edit shows a new number); dates and the date part of every chat order move to the epoch
1700000000 keeping their distances (a 6 h window); the gate's per-run SOCKS5 pair in addedProxy is
blanked and its port written as 1080. tests/fixture.rs reads EVERY testdata/*.jsonl at run time
except login-rules.jsonl (rules, not TDLib output); every RC case compares the stream it sees
with its file (the drift check: phases at the step lines, the set of @type names per phase,
TDLib's own start-up and counter types left out; the refresh types TDLib sends when its own
background fetches return are compared as one set per helper run; in a phase after a kill
updateNewMessage is left out; rec-kill-at-once.jsonl is not compared, it has two outcomes by
design). The test checks that testdata/ holds nothing but this README, string-paths.txt,
login-*.jsonl, rec-*.jsonl, receive-*.jsonl and message-contents.txt. A pin bump re-records. LC-20
(1.8.69) did: every golden assert held on the re-record, 9 of the 19 files came out byte-identical to the 1.8.68
ones and 10 differ only by the version option, a one-second shift of the scrub's dates and orders (the fake world
dates its messages by the wall clock; the distances are kept), the position of TDLib's own option and start-up
bursts, background refreshes of updateSupergroup and updateUserStatus and TDLib's `unix_time` option updates
(sent when its clock difference to the server moves by 0.5 s, so they depend on the fake DC's second boundaries:
timing; one less in four files, one more in two): no new type, key path or string path
(the explanation per file is made by a script that is not committed; .claude/rules/telegram.md, "The recordings").
The files were recorded by the harness as committed here: the kill steps stamped after the stop, the regular RC10
idling 2.5 s after the call push.

rec-read-act.jsonl (TG-4 K2, the sixteenth recording, case RC13 `rc13_read_act`; made and scrubbed like the fifteen above)
is what TDLib wrote while the read act's request (`viewMessages` with `source: null` and `force_read: true`, built by the
product's own `LaneAsk::View`) ran with TDLib's message database off, in two helper runs split by a SIGKILL (step
`kill-after-view`). Steps: `view-private` (the three unread ids of one private chat), `view-supergroup`, `view-again` (the
same ids once more), `view-held` (the fake DC holds read requests: neither applied nor answered, "lost in flight"; one new
message in a private chat and two in a basic group, then a read of the first ones), `kill-after-view` (the second run: lists
loaded again), `reassert` (the private chat's id again: the dialog's top), `reassert-below-top` (the basic group's id below
its top). GOLDEN (TDLib's own behaviour at the pin, re-proven at 1.8.69 by LC-20's re-record, asserted on every run): one `messages.readHistory` for the first, one
`channels.readHistory` for the supergroup, none for the repeat, the `updateChatReadInbox` BEFORE the call's answer, no read
request after the restart before a re-assert (nothing is replayed with the DB off), one request per re-assert, no
`readMessageContents`, views or reactions request. GOLDEN-VIA-MODEL: the `max_id` of each request (read back through the fake
DC's `read-state`, which stores exactly the id a request names). MODEL: the read position TDLib is told after the restart
(below the viewed id because the fake never applied the held read), the answer shapes (`affectedMessages`, `boolTrue`), that
the reading session gets no echo of its own read.

rec-first-live-after-load.jsonl and rec-first-live-before-load.jsonl (TG-4 K3a, the seventeenth and eighteenth
recordings, cases RC12a `rc12a_first_live_after_load` and RC12b `rc12b_first_live_before_load`; made and scrubbed like the
fifteen above) are what TDLib wrote, with its message database off, when the FIRST live message `M` of a chat came after a
restart (B1's case) and what `getChatHistory(chat, from = M, offset 0, limit 50)` (the product's own reconcile request) then
answered, in two helper runs split by a SIGKILL (step `kill-quiet`, after 1 s of quiet so TDLib had saved its pts). Run 1:
the main list loaded, step `tail` (three new messages in each of two chats, one by one: the "lost tail" TDLib processed; the
newest message before them is the stale cursor `s` Kourier would have stored). Run 2 of `-after-load`: step `chats` (the
list loaded again), step `first-live` (`M` of a private chat and of a supergroup), step `pages` (per chat the page from `M`,
then from the oldest id of the last page until an id at or below `s` came). Run 2 of `-before-load`: step `first-live`
straight after the restart (the private chat and a BASIC GROUP, the common box), step `pages`, step `chats`. GOLDEN (TDLib's
own behaviour at the pin, re-proven at 1.8.69 by LC-20's re-record, asserted on every run; U10): in `-after-load` the FIRST page from `M` is TDLib's LOCAL answer, the
chat's top message ALONE, for both chats (after the restart TDLib holds nothing older than the top it was told, so no local page
bridges `M` to the stale cursor; in `-before-load` TDLib holds no top and the first page is the server's whole page, which is
not asserted); a tail message is told again at most once after the restart (it was 0 times in the recording) and `M` exactly
once; a chat's `updateNewChat` comes before `M`'s `updateNewMessage` in run 2. GOLDEN-VIA-MODEL (asserted; valid because the
fake's history is pinned by its unit test `the_history_pages_from_the_newest_by_offset_id_and_add_offset` over `history_ids`,
`fakedc/src/world/answers.rs`): across a chat's pages every tail message comes exactly once, newest first, BEFORE any id at or
below `s`, and `M` is in no page from `M`; that is a fact of the SERVER pages (the next page of `-after-load`, the only page of
`-before-load`), which hold the tail because the fake answers every stored message below the id: R1 reads the real server's
page from `M`, not only the counts. PRINTED, not asserted (timing or MODEL; the recording's run): `-after-load`: that first
page held 1 id with no request on the wire and the next page from it holds the rest (6 ids for the private chat, 3 for the
supergroup), with `updates.getDifference`,
`messages.getPeerDialogs` and `updates.getChannelDifference` all 0 around `M`; `-before-load`: TDLib creates the dialog
from `M` (an `updateNewChat`, an `updateChatReadInbox` with 1 unread and a read position 0, then `M`, corrected by a second
`updateChatReadInbox` with the server's numbers after `messages.getPeerDialogs`: 2 requests, `updates.getDifference` 0 or 1
(printed, timing: 1 in 11 of 28 runs at 1.8.69)) and the FIRST page from `M` is the
server's whole page (7 ids for the private chat, 5 for the basic group). MODEL: the page sizes (the fake answers everything
below the id, up to the limit), the id sequence (one box for private chats and basic groups, one per channel), the
unread numbers, the number of wire requests a call makes.

rec-difference-too-long.jsonl (TG-4 K3c, the nineteenth recording, case RC14 `rc14_difference_too_long`; made and scrubbed like the
fifteen above) is what TDLib wrote, with its message database off, when the COMMON box's `updates.differenceTooLong` came
(the known limit of Q-L12), in two helper runs split by a CLEAN stop (step `stop`, then `restart-too-long`: not a kill). The
fake DC's common too-long threshold is a MODEL choice (`queue threshold common <pts>`): 2 for the restart, then 1 for the live
part. Run 1: the main list loaded, step `stop`. Between the runs four changes are queued (three new messages of a private chat,
one of a basic group: more than the threshold, so the difference from the saved pts is too long). Run 2: step
`restart-too-long` (the lists loaded again, then per chat the page the start decision asks, `getChatHistory(chat, from = the
last message TDLib told)`, the product's own reconcile request), then the live part in the same run: step `live-gap` (two
messages stored and a third pushed: a pts gap of two, the difference is too long again), step `live-next` (the next live
message `M'`), step `live-pages` (`getChatHistory(chat, from M')` once). GOLDEN (TDLib's own behaviour at the pin, re-proven at 1.8.69 by LC-20's re-record, asserted on
every run): at the restart the difference is asked at least twice (the too-long answer sets the pts and TDLib asks again),
NONE of the four queued messages reaches the app as an `updateNewMessage`, and after `loadChats` the last message TDLib tells
for each chat is the newest queued one (`getDialogs` is applied after the difference). GOLDEN-VIA-MODEL (asserted; the fake's
history is pinned by its unit test `the_history_pages_from_the_newest_by_offset_id_and_add_offset`): the server pages from
that last message hold the queued messages once each, newest first, before any id at or below the stale cursor (the fake
answers every stored message below the id; R1 reads the real server's window). Live: a difference is asked after the push (the
recording's run: 2), no object TDLib tells for the rest of the run names one of the three `gap` messages (not as
`updateNewMessage`, not as the chat's last message: the push that revealed the gap does not reach the app either), `M'`
comes exactly once, and the local first page from `M'` starts at the message that was the chat's last BEFORE the gap with none
of the three in it: TDLib bridges the dropped window in its local history (the stated limit's hole; the server's page would
hold them). MODEL: the thresholds, the window sizes, the page sizes, the id sequence (one box for private chats and basic
groups).

receive-<name>.jsonl (TG-3, L-14) are SYNTHETIC lines in TDLib's shapes, written by hand where no
recording holds a kind T3 must read (bot, public, direct-messages and channel chats, scheduled and
pending messages, a re-delivery, the chat updates the fake world never sends; receive-entities.jsonl:
every text entity type, emoji and CJK offsets, broken offsets, mentions, links to example.org;
receive-contents.jsonl: one message per content kind the map reads, line n = message n << 20: media with
remote ids synthetic-remote-<n>, a sticker without ids, places, contacts, polls, view-once and timer media,
protected, album, forwarded, restricted (a synthetic restriction reason) and auto-deleted messages, an unknown content type; receive-service.jsonl:
one message per call and service case the map reads, line n = message n << 20: calls answered or declined
elsewhere and outgoing ones, members added, joined, left and removed, title, photo, timer, create, pin,
payment, video chats, a group call in a private chat, a generic service kind). They hold only the
scrub's synthetic values (Ada Test, Bo Peer, Cy/Di/Ed/Fay Sample, Group <n>, Channel <n>,
synthetic_<n>, "synthetic text <n>", dates within an hour of 1700000000) and are checked like
login-flow.jsonl; every string path they add is on string-paths.txt. They are no golden output of
TDLib: a test never builds on a fact only they hold. message-contents.txt (TG-3 C4a) lists every
MessageContent constructor of the pinned td_api.tl (108 at 1.8.69; 107 at 1.8.68).
